7. Departments with more than 5 employees
SELECT department_id
FROM Employee
GROUP BY department_id
HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;

If asked using a subquery:

SELECT *
FROM Department
WHERE department_id IN
(
    SELECT department_id
    FROM Employee
    GROUP BY department_id
    HAVING COUNT(*) > 5
);
8. EXISTS Subquery ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Customers

Orders

Find customers who placed at least one order.

SELECT *
FROM Customers c
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT *
    FROM Orders o
    WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id
);
9. IN Subquery
SELECT *
FROM Employee
WHERE department_id IN
(
    SELECT department_id
    FROM Department
    WHERE location='Chennai'
);
10. Correlated Subquery ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Employees earning more than their department average.

SELECT *
FROM Employee e
WHERE salary >
(
    SELECT AVG(salary)
    FROM Employee
    WHERE department_id = e.department_id
);

Notice:

e.department_id

comes from the outer query.

This makes it a correlated subquery.

Difference Between IN and EXISTS
IN
SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE id IN
(
    SELECT student_id
    FROM Sports
);

Checks whether a value exists in the list returned by the subquery.

EXISTS
SELECT *
FROM Students s
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT *
    FROM Sports sp
    WHERE sp.student_id = s.id
);

Checks whether the subquery returns at least one row.

Types of Subqueries
Type	Example
Single-row	salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Employee)
Multiple-row	id IN (SELECT student_id FROM Sports)
Correlated	salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Employee WHERE department_id = e.department_id)
EXISTS	WHERE EXISTS (...)
NOT EXISTS	WHERE NOT EXISTS (...)
